    "de": {
      "shared_perspectives": "Im deutschen Mediendiskurs wird trotz unterschiedlicher politischer Ausrichtung ein breiter Konsens darüber deutlich, dass der Fachkräftemangel und die fortschreitende Digitalisierung zentrale Herausforderungen für die Zukunft der Arbeit darstellen und deshalb verstärkte Qualifizierungs‑ und Umschulungsprogramme gefordert werden. Gleichzeitig kritisieren Medien aller Lager Reformen, die die Tarifbindung schwächen oder den Sozialstaat aushöhlen könnten, und betonen die Notwendigkeit, den Mindestlohn und die sozialen Sicherungssysteme zu erhalten, um soziale Stabilität zu gewährleisten.",
      "controversial_points": ""
    },
    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media, regardless of political orientation, repeatedly stress that large‑scale union mobilisations and public protests are the dominant response to government proposals that are framed as cuts to the social safety net or to collective‑bargaining rights. At the same time, there is a cross‑party consensus that digitalisation, artificial‑intelligence deployment and the growth of platform work are fundamentally reshaping the labour market, creating an urgent narrative about the need for systematic reskilling programmes and new regulatory safeguards for workers. Finally, coverage from left‑leaning, centrist and right‑leaning outlets converges on the tension between employer demands for greater flexibility – such as expanded home‑office and gig‑economy models – and the preservation of established worker protections like equal pay, works councils and collective agreements.",
      "controversial_points": "The core conflict pits unions demanding higher wages and collective bargaining against employers advocating flexible contracts and cost control. A second tension divides supporters of an expansive welfare state from market liberals pushing basic income cuts and social benefits."
    }
